00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.080
which means it's going to inform the code and the diffs that we produce.

245
00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:36.280
This means all the code in the repository of itself,

246
00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:39.760
outside of the documentation knowledge base is also prompts.

247
00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:43.640
So if we think about aligning

248
00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:48.200
the code base or unifying it all on the same patterns, we kind of limit

249
00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:52.120
the amount of attention the model needs in order to do a good job.

250
00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:56.120
If I am able to standardize on across my entire stack.

251
00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.120
For example, when the model thinks observable,

252
00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:03.200
it's able to translate context that it sees in one part of the repository

253
00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.240
over to something halfway across the code base

254
00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.160
without any loss of quality or intelligence.

255
00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.000
But if I have six observable stacks in the code base,

256
00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:12.760
the model is going to have

257
00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:16.680
to spend a lot more time figuring out which one do I use here?

258
00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:18.520
Is this migrated or not?

259
00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:20.680
What is canonically good?

260
00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.400
So over the course of the PR, there's

261
00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:28.960
sort of three phases I think about when we're talking about context delivery.

262
00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:34.160
And because we are curating the code base in order to make it efficient

263
00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:37.160
to deliver context to these agents, we also want to encode that

264
00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.200
in the operating loop, we give the model.

265
00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.200
To me, the most important thing that ends up in that agent

266
00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:46.600
file is a numbered set of steps that we expect the model

267
00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:50.200
to go through over every rollout that we do over every session,

268
00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:54.280
we first want it to ground itself in the documentation

269
00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:57.280
knowledge base in the ticket that is proposed.

270
00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.480
We want it to spider through our history of address and design docs,

271
00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:05.320
to figure out how this might impact other features of our code base.

272
00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:09.440
We want it to look at the critical user journeys to inform itself around what

273
00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:14.120
screens and user surfaces are impacted, so it can keep the QA plan in mind.

274
00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:16.120
Over the course of its execution.

275
00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:19.120
We expect some amount of slowness during this process,

276
00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:22.720
because we want to page in all the context around

277
00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:25.800
what it means for this feature to slot in globally.

278
00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:31.520
Then there's sort of a messy middle part of the run where the agent is writing

279
00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:36.640
code, running test, exploring the code base, and for that,

280
00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:41.320
we exploit the fact that these agents are going to call a bunch of tools,

281
00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:45.240
run a bunch of tests in order to use them to just in time, prompt

282
00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:48.360
inject the agent to steer its output back to baseline.

283
00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.560
The tests we write, the lists

284
00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:55.280
we write for agents are very different than the ones that we write for humans.

285
00:16:55.320 --> 00:17:00.600
They by default recognize that agents are going to truncate

286
00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:04.240
tool call outputs, that they respond really well to descriptive error messages

287
00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:07.240
that point them to run books for remediation steps.

288
00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:11.720
And we are willing to have very many of these things

289
00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:12.920
that are kind of fiddly to write.

290
00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:13.960
And I wouldn't normally think

291
00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:17.920
about to go back to this sort of network code example.

292
00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:21.880
I am sure all of you have been paged at some point in your careers

293
00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:25.040
around an outage that boiled down to a missing

294
00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:28.720
timeout and a retry on a cross service network call, and

295
00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.760
the collective amount of engineering time

296
00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:34.720
that has been spent on this very common failure mode is astounding.

297
00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.200
But still today,

298
00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:40.920
like there's there's there's no code that asserts that we pass retries around.

299
00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:44.120
There's no ES lint plugin that I can slot into my code

300
00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:45.680
base that's going to do this for me.

301
00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:48.880
But because the production of code is very, very cheap now,

302
00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:53.840
we can absolutely vibe a set of guardrails into place with 100% code

303
00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.760
coverage and exhaustive table driven tests, and migrate the code base

304
00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:01.920
all in one go and just in time surface this failure to the model

305
00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:05.680
every time it writes another fetch call and never have to worry about this again.

306
00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:10.520
And because we don't have to pollute context window up front,

307
00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:13.840
and we can exploit the fact that a tool called output is going to be

308
00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.160
given less weight during an auto compaction.

309
00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:20.840
We just in time, correct the model and still let it go off

310
00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:24.080
and do the complex work that we wanted to in our original prompt.

311
00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:27.880
And then sort of after the run, we have a much easier

312
00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:30.480
task of determining whether or not the code, the diff,

313
00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:34.320
the artifact is aligned because it's a static thing, and we have static

314
00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:38.160
sets of guardrails and can use very, very many LM

315
00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:41.160
as judge to look at the code,

316
00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:45.160
operationalize it with a set or three of static guardrails.

317
00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.920
This is what it means to write reliable code.

318
00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:52.520
This is what it means to write performant react and make a determination.

319
00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:53.840
Is this good or bad?

320
00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:56.600
And if it's bad, why is it bad?

321
00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:58.960
Because the LMS crave text.

322
00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:03.320
These LMS judges can collaborate with the implementation agent over that

323
00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:06.760
PR thread, give more text back to the implementation agent,

324
00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:10.680
and further realign the proposed diff back to baseline.

325
00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:13.880
So we've got agents

326
00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:17.680
kind of as this map that shows where the context is during

327
00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:21.040
what types of work the model might want to look at that text,

328
00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:24.840
but otherwise not being prescriptive around any of the guardrails.

329
00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.160
We don't want to jam a ton of rules in here,

330
00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.880
because we're going to chop up latent space too much.

331
00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:36.400
We're going to make it difficult for the model to spider through the code base.

332
00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.160
With creativity.

333
00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:42.160
I find it very, very useful from this

334
00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:45.720
to point to a curated set of review

335
00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:49.240
personas that are essentially bulleted list of guardrails.

336
00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:52.960
And I find this really, really neat for an interfacing with the other humans

337
00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:57.000
on the team perspective, because it is so cheap

338
00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:01.560
as a team to have a slack conversation in a thread around

339
00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:02.680
what it means to

340
00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:07.160
fix that performance regression, and then mention the agent in it to say,

341
00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:11.840
you want all of this and put up a PR that adds it to our static set of guardrails.

342
00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:12.760
So cheap.

343
00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:16.440
In order to continually refine and improve the output of our agents in that way.

344
00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.080
I also think it's really neat

345
00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:23.360
to take that same sort of pattern and apply it toward

346
00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:26.960
documenting what your product features are, or what the critical user

347
00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:32.000
journeys are, or why your apps even exist, what user problems they solve.

348
00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:35.200
All this context that we can give the agent helps

349
00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.760
ground it in what we are trying to do and why.

350
00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:41.040
How our team thinks about working.

351
00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:44.640
Because all of this is going to produce more and more aligned output.

352
00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:48.320
In that messy middle.

353
00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:51.840
We can kind of use tests on the AST,

354
00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:57.000
tests on the structure of the files on disk, really blunt

355
00:20:57.040 --> 00:21:01.320
hammers around file line counts, or whether or not snapshot tests exist.

356
00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:04.400
These very, very coarse grained tools, in order

357
00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:07.400
to make the model do what we know is good,

358
00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:10.240
just requiring that every react

359
00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:13.240
component in our codebase has a snapshot test

360
00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:15.960
that gives 100% branch coverage,

361
00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:17.040
means that the model

362
00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:21.080
is naturally decomposing these things and making them pure where possible,

363
00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:25.360
and not doing prop drilling and putting hooks close to where the data is used,

364
00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.040
because that makes it easier for it to fill

365
00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.640
the requirement that there must be snapshot tests.

366
00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.920
And we can do this.

367
00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:35.800
We can assert this because it's free to produce the code that spiders through

368
00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:40.080
disk, and matches up the snapshot test to the underlying component.

369
00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:46.120
Another failure mode that I hear folks talk about a bunch

370
00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:50.720
is that these agents are doing type shaped probing all the time.

371
00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:53.080
I end up with these anys or unknowns all over the code

372
00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:57.360
base, and the way I've approached it is to just statically disallow

373
00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:01.840
any function that has a type of any or unknown, unless it's

374
00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:05.320
pausing input in a root handler or from the database.

375
00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.720
Other than that, with ES lint, we just ban the existence of that type.

376
00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:14.160
We require the code base to be 100% typed, which means all this bad

377
00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:17.760
behavior and weird type probing just kind of falls out.

378
00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.200
Because we require 100% code coverage.

379
00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:24.000
These functions cannot possibly be exercised because the unknown types can

380
00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:28.960
exist, and we get more aligned code, more aligned code that I would consider

381
00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:32.960
acceptable, high quality, maintainable, and all these other sorts of properties.

382
00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:35.080
And having these

383
00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:39.560
failing checks tell the agent why they failed, and what to do instead

384
00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.600
means that it's able to self heal.

385
00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.280
Ultimately, as we move into

386
00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:50.400
that third phase of review and merge, we want to treat the model

387
00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:54.160
as if it's another member of the team and it needs to convince me to merge

388
00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:55.280
its code.

389
00:22:55.280 --> 00:23:00.600
I'm not shoulder surfing any of my teammates in VS code or vim

390
00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.960
when they put up a PR, and they attest that they tested the code,

391
00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:05.720
I take their word, you know?

392
00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:09.840
And if I am unsure, I'll ask them to show me the logs from the staging deploy

393
00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:14.880
or to post a screenshot of them exercising the feature in the app.

394
00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:18.840
And we can require these agents to do the same thing.

395
00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:22.320
It says a lot easier these days.

396
00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:25.600
Now that we have things like computer use and browser use,

397
00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:28.400
the Codex app is fantastic. Highly recommended.

398
00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:32.840
But even without that, you know, vibing yourself up on X corks,

399
00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:37.440
connected headless display in a Docker container and wiring up

400
00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:41.520
FFM peg to that stream to record a reproduction video is within reach.

401
00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:46.120
Because I don't care about how gross this code is, and Codex is able to sling

402
00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:49.280
ffmpeg better than anybody in this room, probably.

403
00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:53.800
On the back half of things

404
00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:58.440
where we are looking for ways to accept the diff.

405
00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.600
I'm treating it again like I would my human teammates benefit of the doubt

406
00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:03.880
biased toward merge.

407
00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:07.160
What are the P2 and above things that would be necessary for me

408
00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:08.720
to accept this code?

409
00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:12.200
Use the reviewer agents, which is really just a matrix CI job

410
00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:16.840
that points out a bunch of markdown files to judge this thing, drive

411
00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:19.880
these remediation to completion,

412
00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:23.280
get the coding agent to pick them up,

413
00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:26.720
implement it, get the reviewers to be happy, and off we go.

414
00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:31.040
And this sort of process with me observing along the way

415
00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:34.040
of which review feedback is regularly getting surfaced.

416
00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:36.600
Why is it making it to this part of the pipeline?

417
00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:37.680
Maybe I need to use

418
00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.680
that as a signal that I need to shift some of these guardrails left,

419
00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:44.120
and then I can spend my time and the reviewer agents can spend

420
00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:47.120
their time on more bespoke or

421
00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:50.480
more complex changes that we need them to look at.

422
00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:53.880
Another thing

423
00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:58.320
that you should be thinking about doing as a team is how to systematize capturing

424
00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:00.000
all of this human feedback,

425
00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.680
every review comment, every time you have had to interrupt the agent,

426
00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:10.240
every agent intervention, every failed build, every exception in production.

427
00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:14.480
All of these, in some sense, are signals that context was missing

428
00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:18.240
to the implementation agent, that it did not consider the full end

429
00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:20.240
to end consequences of the code that it wrote,

430
00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.560
and whether or not it would be successfully deployed.

431
00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:26.560
And what we are trying to do, which I expect you'll learn

432
00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:30.640
about in the next talk, is slurp all this data up and dream over it

433
00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:35.200
every night, pointing a bunch of sub agents at it, trying to distill

434
00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:36.320
whether or not

435
00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:38.000
there's anything that humans can do better

436
00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.320
in their prompting, whether there's missing guardrails

437
00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:41.320
that should exist in the code

438
00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:45.000
base that disallow this behavior, and how we can get to a world where

439
00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:50.040
we're more and more headless, less human, interrupt dependent, and able

440
00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:53.920
to trust the agent to do more and more complex things helplessly.

441
00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:57.440
I think

442
00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:00.440
vibe coding is a big part of what it takes to be successful here,

443
00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.480
because there's a ton of guardrails that only affect my local development process.

444
00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:07.880
This code can be gross, but it brings into possibility

445
00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:11.440
this idea that I don't need

446
00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:14.520
to care about some parts of the software production function.

447
00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:18.560
This lets me operate like a group tech lead or an org lead, where

448
00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:23.440
I don't have visibility into every single engineers activity on the keyboard.

449
00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:27.760
But the thing I care about are invariants, interfaces, whether or not

450
00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:29.280
the components that they're producing

451
00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.280
do what they say on the tin with high reliability.

452
00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:36.200
And with that, I'll just leave it with y'all can go build things.

453
00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:38.840
These tools are fantastic. Go get after it.

454
00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:40.440
I'll take some questions now.

455
00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:44.360
Thank you so much.

